    {
      "id": "848b7p5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "3ajrkow",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "vscuczn",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_vscuczn.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bbyo4v8",
      "content": "Magnetism is an important and frequently asked topic in RRB examinations. This chapter includes magnetic effects of electric current, electromagnetism, electromagnetic induction, Fleming’s rules and electric motor."
    },
    {
      "id": "0jsdoda",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "908rj3d",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_908rj3d.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "hhe980r",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. MAGNET AND MAGNETIC FIELD"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vld9vti",
      "content": "• A magnet has two poles: North and South."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uzcle07",
      "content": "• Like poles repel, unlike poles attract."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v3ha5ha",
      "content": "• Magnetic field is region around magnet where magnetic force is felt."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "oq5ki13",
      "content": "• Field lines emerge from North and enter South (outside magnet)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ez2p77q",
      "content": "• Magnetic field lines never intersect."
    },
    {
      "id": "ttv03uy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. PROPERTIES OF MAGNETIC FIELD LINES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "75khelr",
      "content": "• Form closed continuous curves."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ob0owca",
      "content": "• Closer lines indicate stronger field."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8p4xg65",
      "content": "• Tangent at any point gives direction of field."
    },
    {
      "id": "7c0818k",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. MAGNETIC EFFECT OF ELECTRIC CURRENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k3jgq49",
      "content": "• Discovered by Oersted."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3pzjdmf",
      "content": "• A current-carrying conductor produces magnetic field around it."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0cdejam",
      "content": "• Direction determined by Right Hand Thumb Rule.<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "id": "trn7awx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. RIGHT HAND THUMB RULE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b53xu50",
      "content": "• Hold conductor in right hand. "
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j0qzxui",
      "content": "• Thumb points in direction of current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "coxltrp",
      "content": "• Curled fingers give direction of magnetic field."
    },
    {
      "id": "jhk65q4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. MAGNETIC FIELD DUE TO CIRCULAR COIL"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1grpwmj",
      "content": "• Field at centre increases with number of turns."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h7hox9l",
      "content": "• Increases with current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s7v92fg",
      "content": "• Decreases with radius of coil."
    },
    {
      "id": "3ycf7fl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. SOLENOID AND ELECTROMAGNET"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gey9qi8",
      "content": "• Solenoid: Long coil of insulated wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wnmeqon",
      "content": "• Produces uniform magnetic field inside."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v8kc9dt",
      "content": "• Electromagnet: Soft iron core placed inside solenoid."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1i95jvy",
      "content": "• Strength depends on current and number of turns."
    },
    {
      "id": "bomwyn2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. FORCE ON CURRENT CARRYING CONDUCTOR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6ayk1mz",
      "content": "• When conductor placed in magnetic field, it experiences force."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "urle4pm",
      "content": "• Direction given by Fleming’s Left Hand Rule."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sm1a2vx",
      "content": "• Basis of electric motor."
    },
    {
      "id": "a88uy14",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. FLEMING’S LEFT HAND RULE"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "u8zq2s7",
      "content": "• Forefinger → Magnetic field"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5utmphx",
      "content": "• Middle finger → Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bp841w7",
      "content": "• Thumb → Force (motion)<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "id": "ndvo671",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9. ELECTRIC MOTOR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ve33b7w",
      "content": "• Converts electrical energy into mechanical energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nw4l8hi",
      "content": "• Works on principle of force on current-carrying conductor."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2hvx9qq",
      "content": "• Components: Armature, Commutator, Brushes, Field magnet."
    },
    {
      "id": "e6ol1vn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "10. ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j6owsvc",
      "content": "• Discovered by Faraday."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "62nmklj",
      "content": "• Change in magnetic field induces current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a04hzei",
      "content": "• Induced current direction given by Fleming’s Right Hand Rule."
    },
    {
      "id": "b3qhbyg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "11. FLEMING’S RIGHT HAND RULE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ptiv262",
      "content": "• Thumb → Motion of conductor"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "3qkzi2d",
      "content": "• Forefinger → Magnetic field"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mcecupo",
      "content": "• Middle finger → Induced current"
    },
    {
      "id": "ih8g74m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "12. ELECTRIC GENERATOR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vggpwnn",
      "content": "• Converts mechanical energy into electrical energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lrbtyvk",
      "content": "• Based on electromagnetic induction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g20scqv",
      "content": "• AC generator uses slip rings."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hud29zr",
      "content": "• DC generator uses split ring (commutator)."
    },
    {
      "id": "ybv9aqe",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. DOMESTIC ELECTRIC CIRCUIT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "irmq01t",
      "content": "• Live wire (Red), Neutral (Black), Earth (Green)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7o5xqfm",
      "content": "• Fuse connected in live wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dbam6my",
      "content": "• Appliances connected in parallel."
    },
    {
      "id": "val8plx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "14. MAGNETIC FIELD STRENGTH (B ∝ I / r)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "er55ywc",
      "content": "When current flows through a straight conductor, a magnetic field is produced around it. The strength of magnetic field depends on current and distance."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v5rpt28",
      "content": "• B ∝ I (Directly proportional to current)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ffqvx4o",
      "content": "• B ∝ 1/r (Inversely proportional to distance)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2fto3mq",
      "content": "Combined relation: B ∝ I / r"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hdylsh4",
      "content": "If current doubles → Field doubles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "guxhl8j",
      "content": "If distance doubles → Field becomes half."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "11x5n05",
      "content": "Important: It follows 1/r relation, not 1/r²."
    },
    {
      "id": "hspo5qp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "15. ELECTROMAGNET VS PERMANENT MAGNET"
    },
    {
      "id": "vzdgx0k",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Electromagnet</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Permanent Magnet</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Magnetism</p>",
            "<p>Temporary</p>",
            "<p>Permanent</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Control</p>",
            "<p>Can be switched ON/OFF</p>",
            "<p>Cannot be switched off</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Core Material</p>",
            "<p>Soft Iron</p>",
            "<p>Steel/Hard material</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mig93we",
      "content": "Soft iron is used because it gets magnetized and demagnetized easily."
    },
    {
      "id": "2ti28i7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "16. DIRECTION OF MAGNETIC FIELD (RIGHT HAND THUMB RULE)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5634irx",
      "content": "Thumb → Direction of current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fxmgbzv",
      "content": "Fingers → Direction of magnetic field."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "md4un9x",
      "content": "Current upward → Field anticlockwise."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yr37rtc",
      "content": "Current downward → Field clockwise."
    },
    {
      "id": "gzf8y55",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "17. TORQUE ON CURRENT LOOP"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zjh5qjr",
      "content": "When a current carrying loop is placed in magnetic field, opposite forces act on opposite sides."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8s2sd4p",
      "content": "These forces create torque which causes rotation."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k59yf1n",
      "content": "Principle of Electric Motor."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "silji67",
      "content": "Greater current → Greater torque."
    },
    {
      "id": "dhzbwwk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "18. AC VS DC CURRENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "23hi3l8",
      "content": "DC: Flows in one direction. Example: Battery."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ivoy3es",
      "content": "AC: Changes direction periodically. Household supply is AC (50 Hz in India)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "40tteya",
      "content": "AC graph is sinusoidal. DC graph is straight line."
    },
    {
      "id": "1e5yn5z",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "29. FLEMING’S RULE COMPARISON"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gd91ln1",
      "content": "Left Hand Rule → Used in Electric Motor (Force)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ee7ubct",
      "content": "Right Hand Rule → Used in Generator (Induced Current)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bzsmtn4",
      "content": "Memory Trick: LEFT = MOTOR, RIGHT = GENERATOR."
    },
    {
      "id": "oblmtd3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "20. ADVANCED RRB EXAM POINTS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "inxo31i",
      "content": "• Magnetic field inside solenoid is uniform."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Electromagnet",
        "Permanent Magnet"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Nature of Magnetism",
          "Temporary (active only when current flows)",
          "Permanent"
        ],
        [
          "Core Material Used",
          "Soft Iron",
          "Steel / Alnico / Nipermag"
        ],
        [
          "Magnetic Field Strength",
          "Variable (controlled by varying current or turns)",
          "Fixed and constant"
        ],
        [
          "Polarity",
          "Reversible (by reversing current direction)",
          "Fixed poles"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "amvnub5"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uljjquc",
      "content": "• Earth behaves like a giant magnet."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "13tv39z",
      "content": "• Soft iron used in electromagnets."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "idad3g5",
      "content": "• Fleming’s left rule → Motor."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "obineh7",
      "content": "• Fleming’s right rule → Generator."
    },
    {
      "id": "slb85iv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "MCQs"
    },
    {
      "id": "dbks98c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q1."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "st3u1cw",
      "content": "The magnetic field around a straight current-carrying conductor is directly proportional to:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ccxu4vr",
      "content": "A) Square of current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "mwxtvsc",
      "content": "B) Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ky8zl1q",
      "content": "C) Square of distance"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5wxa9co",
      "content": "D) Inverse square of current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2aiu5vf",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: B (Current)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v8lzur4",
      "content": "<br></br>Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "30lmbd4",
      "content": "Magnetic field strength follows relation B ∝ I / r."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u6php1e",
      "content": "It increases directly with current and decreases with distance."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gpbei47",
      "content": "Therefore, it is directly proportional to current."
    },
    {
      "id": "7rdti1y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qg43j37",
      "content": "Which material is used as core in an electromagnet?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "htigwqx",
      "content": "A) Steel"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "944v59p",
      "content": "B) Copper"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "kyximyz",
      "content": "C) Soft iron"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "i91t3em",
      "content": "D) Aluminium"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "airpj5b",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: C (Soft iron)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ok5yw14",
      "content": "<br></br>Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m272ldg",
      "content": "Soft iron is used because it is easily magnetized and demagnetized."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "210mdmf",
      "content": "Electromagnet needs temporary magnetism."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aknikxx",
      "content": "Steel retains magnetism and is not suitable."
    },
    {
      "id": "t667z43",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q3."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rzxu6qg",
      "content": "Fleming’s Left Hand Rule is used to determine:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "i4zjea0",
      "content": "A) Direction of induced current"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jvbr665",
      "content": "B) Direction of force on conductor"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "k3j1i70",
      "content": "C) Direction of magnetic field"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vl1ru1s",
      "content": "D) Direction of electric field"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gdr5a3p",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: B (Direction of force on conductor)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bp3zpeq",
      "content": "<br></br>Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5j4auws",
      "content": "Fleming’s Left Hand Rule applies to electric motor."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "73d8xln",
      "content": "Thumb → Force (motion)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "3odtx67",
      "content": "Forefinger → Magnetic field"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Motor vs Generator Rule: Use Fleming's LEFT-Hand Rule for Electric Motors (Force/Motion) and Fleming's RIGHT-Hand Rule for Electric Generators (Induced Current).",
        "Direction of Field Lines: Outside a magnet, field lines move from North to South; inside a magnet/solenoid, they move from South to North.",
        "Domestic Circuit Specs: In India, domestic electric supply is standard AC at 220 V and 50 Hz."
      ],
      "id": "07n7woo"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "abnyr0d",
      "content": "Middle finger → Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gndfqzx",
      "content": "It gives direction of motion of conductor."
    },
    {
      "id": "mflgm0o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q4."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "26djqtm",
      "content": "Household electric supply in India is:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uyo6t2o",
      "content": "A) DC"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "anmd2ho",
      "content": "B) AC"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0wnr94r",
      "content": "C) Pulsating DC"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ycnq067",
      "content": "D) Static current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "4pu9fce",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: B (AC)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wxlanv1",
      "content": "<br></br>Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3cwvpq6",
      "content": "Household electricity is Alternating Current (AC)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nohus8r",
      "content": "Frequency in India is 50 Hz."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hlfvo5g",
      "content": "Battery provides DC, not household supply."
    },
    {
      "id": "dxeb8zh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q5."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ocbywec",
      "content": "Electric generator works on the principle of:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xhsk615",
      "content": "A) Heating effect of current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "b0x4vo7",
      "content": "B) Electromagnetic induction"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uyo36at",
      "content": "C) Chemical reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "kudcfye",
      "content": "D) Static electricity"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "s94k9hx",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: B (Electromagnetic induction)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3lxqzjx",
      "content": "<br></br>Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5mahhjz",
      "content": "Electric generator works on Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qtmhzcv",
      "content": "Change in magnetic field induces current in conductor."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kpbq9sm",
      "content": "Direction of induced current is given by Fleming’s Right Hand Rule."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}